Step 1 — src/core/power_calculator.{h,cpp} + esp32-electricity-counter.ino
  Status: PASS
  Build: arduino-cli compile --fqbn esp32:esp32:esp32s3:FlashSize=4M,
    PartitionScheme=no_fs,CDCOnBoot=cdc --warnings all -> 1,463,653 B (72%)
    flash / 116,408 B (35%) RAM. Only pre-existing -Wformat banner warning.
  Changes:
    - collectSamples(): per-sample EMA removed; currentSamples now raw analogRead.
    - computeAll(): after noise-floor quadrature subtraction, apply EMA on the
      RMS estimate: filteredCurrentRMS += alpha*(signalRMS - filteredCurrentRMS),
      alpha=1.0 bypass. New bool rmsInit[] seeds the filter on first use.
    - setLpfAlpha()/setNoiseFloor() reset rmsInit so a changed alpha re-initializes.
    - auto-zero: .ino now routes lpf force/restore through setLpfAlpha() so rmsInit
      resets; runAutoZeroSingle still reads raw currentRMS (floor=0, lpf=1.0).
  Why: time-domain EMA before RMS attenuates the fundamental (alpha=0.01 -> ~64%
    at 50Hz). Filtering the post-RMS estimate smooths readings without touching
    amplitude. Bonus: activePower cross-correlation now uses raw V+I samples, so
    real power/PF are phase-accurate when alpha<1.0.
  Pipeline: ADC raw -> RMS -> cal + noise-floor -> EMA LPF -> output.

